#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162082 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161895 :question/id #uuid "fdab3229-66ca-59d3-ac20-8a5b3d3506fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-9471" :question/problem "![](images/84/84de7754ed9244e3d646e452f6ed6dab349c130d17eae1d4dbc94cb52bd761e1.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above, traversed once counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/f4/f4e80a23910612bd55e7578a074f23c76535139889faff479c4dd0521e7496ff.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C = C_1 \\cup C_2 \\cup C_3$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{C_1}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_2}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s + \\int_{C_3}f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nThe path $C_1$ is a semicircle of radius $r = 3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} \\,\\textrm ds = \\dfrac{1}{2}(2 \\pi r) = 3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe paths $C_2$ and $C_3$ are hypotenuses of right triangles whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} \\,\\textrm ds = \\int_{C_3} \\,\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = 3\\pi + 5 + 5 = 3\\pi + 10.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e60b3dd1-0842-562d-a72f-5adb6a60cba8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050975 :topic/math-academy-id 459 :topic/title "The Distance Formula"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac6987e-1443-5102-be6c-7c404cb81a29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi +1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253751} {:answer/id #uuid "4848d525-4fed-5241-aa55-e8be28361018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\pi +2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254286} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb762e8-b607-526d-970c-769c2e97b12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi +2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254526} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ab8300-d36e-5b2f-b815-bf8f3c00e075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2\\pi +1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255094} {:answer/id #uuid "f438f0f4-4512-5533-b108-0d1c4c07ad3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\pi +1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fb762e8-b607-526d-970c-769c2e97b12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi +2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254526} :answer-field/id #uuid "02208297-3b6c-56a2-86c8-c39f569c53b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255921}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef2f2e1e-2df6-5eea-a523-f7500a9f7301" :question/math-academy-id "q-122481" :question/problem "![](images/55/558dbbc424725ca0d9bad628968489cc2e32d67136102290666ff055a634bc16.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $2$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/82/82fedadbb33b1747832bac25d9cda8e0b6b18b0fab411a870e90bc09ce47226e.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a semi-circle of radius $r=2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\pi r=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is a line segment of length $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\pi +4=2(\\pi +2).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "976609f5-9b7b-5cf5-8af9-7eda58f599e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2} + \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159171} {:answer/id #uuid "f5827111-0006-5c1b-ba8a-12b199fc5f54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π + 2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159172} {:answer/id #uuid "1e127cda-f337-5d92-a559-58f2b9959529" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π + \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159173} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7e980f-41b3-58a9-b313-eb4e1299df8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2} + \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159174} {:answer/id #uuid "68d2396c-897e-51b5-9821-0211bcf63840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π + 3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159175}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9788a76e-e0cc-5a5d-b125-0a5dce44952b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159170}] :question/id #uuid "e7cfc0f1-43a0-55ae-a985-5e79d8a25ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-122484" :question/problem "![](images/52/5277642b981910a5da1dd00a1b9aa4db28c7db7fcacf33faf209eea3ec1e93ab.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}ds$, where $C$ is a path that traverses a circular segment of radius $2$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461a27ae-9bae-56e4-a290-8a9b99dd2f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254262} {:answer/id #uuid "48f7a64a-5065-5b48-9afa-0c3ec86372c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254296} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa5f20e-2e73-5f1c-9c2f-9b1fb94075b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254799} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6906ae-426e-569c-adc9-44144d8e41a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254901} {:answer/id #uuid "a3313bd1-e6b1-51a4-ba38-134097b446aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fa5f20e-2e73-5f1c-9c2f-9b1fb94075b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254799} :answer-field/id #uuid "37cd5012-6b1d-57b4-a60a-c7978b2ab3f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256019}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23524215-604c-5fbd-8acf-4af9e94c29ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-122828" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a7cb0b31cb5d906803063597ae968be8fba90f048a4d9745225de7c3f8d6db.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of three parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/05/058c7b33112329df321e61660df4f2d388f42ca40c0aae5b73a089a0251726e2.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe paths ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are line segments of length $3.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3+5+3=11.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48070546-d80f-5246-86a4-aa8d7aa99c50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254279} {:answer/id #uuid "72a7d010-8a49-501c-a8b0-6c4408fdf75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254685} {:answer/id #uuid "96002547-d724-56a8-8e09-188ad7369cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254992} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1a64fc-def5-5882-88a7-756a23766b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255336} {:answer/id #uuid "c24b4875-c3c0-55e6-b7fe-e4f60d7affdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a7d010-8a49-501c-a8b0-6c4408fdf75f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254685} :answer-field/id #uuid "01fb92ac-5f7f-5736-aab9-a7276e24a517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255920}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f87f9aea-995d-5fa2-9903-84aef89b9be9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122843" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7844777c0bb540ba337756f45c4d71a8a8e9f9c84372e01542892801efecce3.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/98/98f444851e2df505d4242d1f8e4be0d76f78babd5d9633d9ab0ec84c245fe364.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is three-quarters of a circle of radius $r=2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{3}{4}(2\\pi r)=3\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is a line segment of length $2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=3\\pi +2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2461d307-3086-5a5c-b497-aa48ad65c133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253997} {:answer/id #uuid "2dbd98bc-e62d-5a92-bfa0-9c4e1787b547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254082} {:answer/id #uuid "65e5a51e-ba13-5ae9-a263-385c9c9cf5de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(3+\\sqrt{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254582} {:answer/id #uuid "a1016a1d-d5a6-5066-9d4a-cb08a1cf0136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255084} {:answer/id #uuid "b2237b88-2d33-5dfc-a776-cc65d79f2e63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1016a1d-d5a6-5066-9d4a-cb08a1cf0136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255084} :answer-field/id #uuid "a64d136c-2e50-5fe9-8693-5b8666a0d0be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256225}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "efd8621e-7cbb-5240-b849-bfa0535c815e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122845" :question/problem "![](images/68/68b3f78c29c9d7776efc2a78672ae53fe2e806d7ca6c3d663de8a16c03342132.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/27/27c19b56a24315f1105539afc441a33957dddf606b3410b86121e7cb8ed16973.png)\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $3$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs have lengths $2$ and $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=5+2\\sqrt{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06fbccd3-1730-54fc-9475-ad997c5afc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253721} {:answer/id #uuid "21e53e95-7ec4-5c95-b9e3-453c9a2ed4f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2\\pi +\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253974} {:answer/id #uuid "44332bd0-327e-53e0-9733-797aa948733d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254249} {:answer/id #uuid "6169a709-a992-502e-9cbc-f73991286538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254543} {:answer/id #uuid "819bf68a-c423-5c96-b729-706e6eb1fc6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "819bf68a-c423-5c96-b729-706e6eb1fc6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254817} :answer-field/id #uuid "4639a58d-2fdb-5f66-8101-7eaad8afbbd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256053}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65d60feb-29ba-53eb-9de0-464deb1c4c60" :question/math-academy-id "q-122850" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf87c4707c0f4a8ab1b5157a13e8c7785da915833c5f9d896a2747af27f70e59.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above." :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/ff/ff52b8e83a9cc9867b25ea3c24ed0f988a27dec73f9e0ab2dfda28ddabb3238a.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $4.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=2\\pi +4\\sqrt{2}=2(\\pi +2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Line Integrals Over Piecewise-Smooth Curves"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162083 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161896 :question/id #uuid "4d5a8f9d-7324-547c-ae5e-b49294debc1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9472" :question/problem "Consider the curves $C_1$ and $C_2,$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\frac{1}{9}{t}^{3},\\,t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 3 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨3{u}^{3},\\,3u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = 3k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k$" :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Notice that $C_1$ and $C_2$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that $C_1$ and $C_2$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{9}y^3, \\quad 0\\leq y\\leq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds  = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm ds = k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96d48adf-a293-54ae-88fd-ee8471e5fc65" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049144 :topic/math-academy-id 1255 :topic/title "Cartesian Equations of Parametric Curves"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8de278d-ef94-53b3-a37d-10ade572c491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159159} {:answer/id #uuid "db1ebdeb-d8c2-5ee3-bc75-4945f7199fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159160} {:answer/id #uuid "feddb339-fa62-5dad-8d5b-8385d20d7d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159161} {:answer/id #uuid "02c1b5cc-f5dd-5df3-aa07-49600ed22732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159162} {:answer/id #uuid "75d4187a-0ea4-5604-bfd9-c405fec10a64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159163}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4f88280b-5b73-509d-b173-5a9d26ec9781" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159158}] :question/id #uuid "03eb9f6c-5710-543d-af91-9b267957eccb" :question/math-academy-id "q-122478" :question/problem "Suppose $\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = 2$ for some function $f(x, y)$, where $C_{1}$ is parametrized by $r_{1}(t) = ⟨t, t^{2}⟩$ for $0 \\le t \\le 1$.\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)ds =-2$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = 2$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}f(x, y)ds = 4$, where $C_{2}$ is parametrized by $r_{2}(u) = ⟨2u, 4u^{2}⟩$ for $0 \\le u \\le \\frac{1}{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95ed31a0-aafb-51f7-ab3d-e8ff5050b401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159183} {:answer/id #uuid "9e77d5a5-ebf9-5dce-9e8c-ff031c4ef20a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159184} {:answer/id #uuid "a966c171-289b-5056-80b1-a3a81a3dff1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159185} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee86563-d77d-5365-bfcf-19085bedd792" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159186} {:answer/id #uuid "299d46ee-11e3-5c2a-8729-11f0f4e10517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159187}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc74610-e88e-565b-911f-e91506b49f46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159182}] :question/id #uuid "c6541032-7a7a-5a1c-bb3c-d0a7d3bd3d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122571" :question/problem "Consider the curves $C_{1}$ and $C_{2}$, defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}C_{1}:r_{1}(t)|= ⟨\\sin t, \\cos t⟩, & 0 \\le t \\le 2π \\\\ C_{2}:r_{2}(θ)|= ⟨\\sin 2θ, \\cos 2θ⟩, & 0 \\le θ \\le π\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow suppose $\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = k$ for some function $f(x, y)$ and real number $k$. Which of the following must be true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)ds = k$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}f(x, y)ds = k$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{2}}f(x, y)ds = \\frac{1}{2}k$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b877064-2518-5f0a-b4cc-e8ce3cdb6339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462061} {:answer/id #uuid "d31ab5cc-8f03-565c-baa7-182f415d2e40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b877064-2518-5f0a-b4cc-e8ce3cdb6339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462061} :answer-field/id #uuid "a889bc2a-6c37-5857-b5df-2f84c3f8a657" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462784} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4072d32e-8787-592f-a6a0-ab69807057af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462063} {:answer/id #uuid "119a56a7-be8a-525d-bf38-85a10b27e983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4072d32e-8787-592f-a6a0-ab69807057af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462063} :answer-field/id #uuid "115eacf4-2515-53aa-bfd6-ab8ce878fbab" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462785} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac676307-c5cf-59c4-8590-312fc31f883c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462065} {:answer/id #uuid "298a07b4-50d6-5be9-96f1-5114af5fa7ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "298a07b4-50d6-5be9-96f1-5114af5fa7ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462066} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e4091b-8534-5001-a586-9ba94089c8a0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462786}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b1e210e-8810-5093-9deb-eb8812e9311b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122814" :question/problem "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 2.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-4$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=8,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨16{u}^{4}-12{u}^{2},\\,2u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx={y}^{4}-3{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d69ae25e-4067-57d7-992a-aea277568ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462067} {:answer/id #uuid "738c6bee-a025-5815-b191-c51eede9211c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d69ae25e-4067-57d7-992a-aea277568ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462067} :answer-field/id #uuid "c61b6f96-e8f3-53f9-b0c0-da26da3e6072" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4efe198-1a08-51cc-bb7f-57e8f0ee8eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462069} {:answer/id #uuid "51e47506-7d0a-5b3e-b515-61f96ae37f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4efe198-1a08-51cc-bb7f-57e8f0ee8eb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462069} :answer-field/id #uuid "5edbd1d2-7f0f-56a6-b340-a1ec1e119077" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462788} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bba662d-b093-5629-b0a1-c818e13f06a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462071} {:answer/id #uuid "cad3636b-9235-5c64-8894-54bc8fd1a1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cad3636b-9235-5c64-8894-54bc8fd1a1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462072} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3bdd892-fdc2-556f-bf24-620230aff8e4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "533221ac-cdcc-53a5-adcd-03f57c6b1b57" :question/math-academy-id "q-122815" :question/problem "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(u)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{u}{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{u}{2})⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 2\\pi .$ \n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-1$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we note that both parameterizations traverse a half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ between $0$ and $\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e05ec3e-cd64-5efe-b86c-3a85d32445a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462073} {:answer/id #uuid "555ded60-6e27-5822-bfad-111ae26de821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "555ded60-6e27-5822-bfad-111ae26de821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462074} :answer-field/id #uuid "570b3d91-151c-5000-a5f6-67a9b778db1d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce53e294-37f1-5e16-892e-9c87da353bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462075} {:answer/id #uuid "2fecce07-97ed-543c-9956-f750f8ecd7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce53e294-37f1-5e16-892e-9c87da353bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462075} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c544999-5092-5e3f-ba7a-7f9b6e161fa2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462791} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d78c6e8e-5d8a-5dfc-b896-579c21a26b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462077} {:answer/id #uuid "60092f97-7805-566d-a27b-a35ee30d9698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d78c6e8e-5d8a-5dfc-b896-579c21a26b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462077} :answer-field/id #uuid "6319433e-b1e6-54c4-9ece-69e71614667d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462792}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aba31fa9-c0e0-5ac2-bdfd-ad58b74f6f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-122816" :question/problem "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 4.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-3$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=12,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨4u,\\,8{u}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=3.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny={x}^{3/2},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "756cdc53-2932-5002-93f3-0e049675c868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462079} {:answer/id #uuid "c481b00c-7230-5985-9e66-4d8b6087f4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "756cdc53-2932-5002-93f3-0e049675c868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462079} :answer-field/id #uuid "a52e79c6-6b76-598b-a0c0-137e3101dc68" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462793} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be94117-938f-5e8d-b8dc-23f5959ac80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462081} {:answer/id #uuid "d62869e1-04c3-5ef3-b5f6-f5bbc804f3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0be94117-938f-5e8d-b8dc-23f5959ac80d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462081} :answer-field/id #uuid "da261af8-d4c1-56bf-8d02-1b93eb8f0515" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462794} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d76ae1f0-3cf0-5980-8be4-9864329b561e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462083} {:answer/id #uuid "668a58f5-744b-5464-a0b3-4a5197e2f210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "668a58f5-744b-5464-a0b3-4a5197e2f210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462084} :answer-field/id #uuid "b55baf9c-63c9-5110-ad82-91c7b4d263f7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462795}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "67eba06b-e142-51bc-86cf-6cd55545886d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122851" :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+1},\\,3t⟩, & 0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1},\\,2u⟩, & 0\\le u\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-k$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{2k}{3}$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1},\\,0\\le y\\le 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d626c071-0c70-52ac-bc79-16a2a3cf5cad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462085} {:answer/id #uuid "db1e1ca4-1a4a-5645-912d-ad584f2b5ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db1e1ca4-1a4a-5645-912d-ad584f2b5ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462086} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f97f6d4-1cc7-5a18-9002-6bc12e80f56c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462796} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8ec0988-8d3b-58bb-8f11-a31026537190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462087} {:answer/id #uuid "7122ea47-8dea-506b-a7e9-89375fe1971b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8ec0988-8d3b-58bb-8f11-a31026537190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462087} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eccce40-3459-5071-bdd9-96e4fe102f63" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462797} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd90f72-67c5-5198-8924-c359511557f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462089} {:answer/id #uuid "97613da4-4123-53e9-ac7f-e007a645737b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97613da4-4123-53e9-ac7f-e007a645737b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462090} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e9d11d-7777-5fe2-9ce3-8596ef406783" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462798}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "261c6852-f708-5ef6-9e89-3d88f4dbba8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122868" :question/problem "Consider the curves ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2},$ defined parametrically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\,{r}_{1}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,2\\sqrt{t}⟩, & 0\\le t\\le 2 \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\,{r}_{2}(u) & =⟨4u,\\,2\\sqrt{2u}⟩, & 0\\le u\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$ for some function $f(x,y)$ and real number $k.$ Which of the following must be true?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=2k$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x},\\,0\\le x\\le 4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Independence of Parametrization and Orientation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179670 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179525 :question/id #uuid "cd73b214-d887-5dbb-93f9-b71e16711a24" :question/math-academy-id "e-6304" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y) = e^{x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,3)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=3\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\,\\mathbf{i}+3\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =3\\,j+t[(2\\,i+3\\,j)-3\\,j] \\\\  & =3\\,j+t(2\\,i) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =2\\,i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = e^{x+y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{2t+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+3,$ $\\textrm d u=2\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & ={e}^{5}-{e}^{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{3}({e}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11fb303b-053a-56d1-9b96-acd75c748b51" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1c650e8-11a0-52eb-872a-b80e91ee30fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178607} {:answer/id #uuid "d35a1bfb-6883-5741-a942-a7daa5cf9a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178608} {:answer/id #uuid "05cb2099-ecaa-5c5f-9f08-bbfc784a05c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178609} {:answer/id #uuid "1b0ccd47-0269-52a7-b9f5-d64377f51a7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178610} {:answer/id #uuid "1fbf8a9d-e82a-5376-8c65-f152b09d8847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178611}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d8e4dc0d-67c6-574f-9ac0-06e759852449" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178606}] :question/id #uuid "48c3ffb7-0c2f-51cd-baed-2f769ffcd563" :question/math-academy-id "q-39954" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = 2xy$ along the line segment from $(1, 2)$ to $(2, 1)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180281 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "908ad41e-6b90-53c7-a0b0-1379b101bcc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179231} {:answer/id #uuid "cddf3bc2-f4f4-5b96-9207-d2a635343e76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179232} {:answer/id #uuid "05bc89cf-d69a-58a1-8b47-152158c35292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179233} {:answer/id #uuid "3d3ec1ec-5a8b-5453-bd05-2a07d125bb8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179234} {:answer/id #uuid "465dc9bc-ab17-545e-a45e-f7d0b63a0a54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179235}] :answer-field/id #uuid "53b18fe0-468e-5cc2-be74-8e5b87b85f7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179230}] :question/id #uuid "5add58ef-e981-55d7-918e-05a8761bd0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-76565" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x, y) = 2x - 3y$ along the line segment from $(1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0)$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259517 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f23753-d48c-590e-bac5-29003f108849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256826} {:answer/id #uuid "0ec75479-032b-576f-8363-7dd3f604bf87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256923} {:answer/id #uuid "9fc17017-3d39-5a47-a04e-8e25c1e82e70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258184} {:answer/id #uuid "b6215184-663f-5e94-a809-e223798544a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258365} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0bd4a7-bd12-5381-bd48-060bce75559a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb0bd4a7-bd12-5381-bd48-060bce75559a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258422} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba54ee22-09fd-52a5-9f4c-6aad923451fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259354}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed909600-8f3f-5028-beb2-725ed7978fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123363" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$ along the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,i$ and $b=2\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,i+t[(2\\,i+2\\,j)-2\\,i] \\\\  & =2\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2$ and $y=2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)j \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{2y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2+\\sqrt{2(2t)} \\\\  & =2+2\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+2\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\sqrt{t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t+\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1+\\frac{2}{3})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b297174-db28-5631-8ce7-0b6db400048f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257165} {:answer/id #uuid "527ae6e8-f296-5d07-9bf7-e7e1c033101a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257488} {:answer/id #uuid "536119d2-aa2d-5141-9ba4-6effa0c108f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257493} {:answer/id #uuid "cef321a9-d7a9-52b7-b902-74641ba92360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258579} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e2f493-4761-5d47-968b-8b6a4da2f392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}({e}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef321a9-d7a9-52b7-b902-74641ba92360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258579} :answer-field/id #uuid "429e5a21-6fd3-5106-9c69-da9de1dbca48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf9ff7ae-6e8a-5ec6-8ed3-2941337baefb" :question/math-academy-id "q-123366" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$ along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-1,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t\\,(b) \\\\  & =t(-i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{3x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{3(-t)+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}[{e}^{t}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b4c92cd-b35d-5c18-a223-49150a03011a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\sqrt{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342724} {:answer/id #uuid "9854cc39-fd63-5ffa-8334-c3108e197497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343574} {:answer/id #uuid "a719e5db-f272-5b05-bc3d-faa57d00c911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343708} {:answer/id #uuid "ac56b14e-40b3-5364-ac94-8f30ec9d2137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343754} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f2338d-321a-5c08-ba22-d59d140ae37a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9854cc39-fd63-5ffa-8334-c3108e197497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343574} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe61a69d-0a4f-5fcc-b1ef-4f5a0dca0429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bfd19a9-a685-5ea5-8c9a-dcf991921379" :question/math-academy-id "q-76564" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+7j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[4\\,i+7j-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(3i+5j) \\\\  & =(1+3t)\\,i+(2+5t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+3t$ and $y=2+5t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+5t)j \\\\  & =3i+5j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+3t)(2+5t) \\\\  & =15{t}^{2}+11t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{34}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{[5{t}^{3}+\\frac{11}{2}{t}^{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}(5+\\frac{11}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0917ea-40c5-507b-8924-1137e4b9229d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342322} {:answer/id #uuid "28556a83-5674-5632-a7b0-74ac4c5a6211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342576} {:answer/id #uuid "356c398e-4bd2-59df-a741-dfdcb21b6a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342678} {:answer/id #uuid "5abb0cb1-003f-5e6f-b8df-beba529f73a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342996} {:answer/id #uuid "a44d1448-a188-5bd6-bad3-7888bafa60d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a0917ea-40c5-507b-8924-1137e4b9229d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342322} :answer-field/id #uuid "96bfae9f-8994-5c06-b70f-c7f375df05f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344753}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2db69cc9-2b8f-5c01-a877-0e37bcf27dc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76605" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+j)+t[2\\,i+4\\,j-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(i+j)+t(i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(1+3t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t$ and $y=1+3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)j \\\\  & =1\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi ((1+3t)-(1+t))}{8}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{\\pi t}{4},$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9d0ea6-699e-529f-8386-4d8ae6eb9a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e\\sqrt{2}({e}^{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342592} {:answer/id #uuid "530ca827-8329-5ca0-99fc-22a829724023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342933} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e2be51-e86b-58ab-9a10-7841de2f4293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344151} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe6f6f8-75fd-55fd-9a9a-45090e8a563d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344202} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dafa4e-91ab-5e93-a865-b1e60c56b9cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9e2be51-e86b-58ab-9a10-7841de2f4293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344151} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3865ea-0943-5557-a486-c9bc91ea15eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344678}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2968de0-3f24-5e15-95ba-b17804d225c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-76650" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s$ along the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t[2\\,i+3\\,j-j] \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4(2t)+2){e}^{2t(1+2t)} \\\\  & =(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+4{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=(2+8t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179586 :question/id #uuid "b7d08596-fbe4-5601-a58e-dac90715f7e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9486" :question/problem "Find a definite integral that is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+t[(i+j)-i] \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =0\\,i+j \\\\  & =j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "189f51d6-494f-515a-bf77-59496421b093" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485 :topic/math-academy-id 1376 :topic/title "The Vector Equation of a Line"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050289 :topic/math-academy-id 2107 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c16fe8e-df28-5acf-80d4-30daf328aacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253906} {:answer/id #uuid "5aca3d77-fbac-59a8-a8e8-f141fffb748d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254472} {:answer/id #uuid "6641d652-1c79-5594-8a40-67438b550538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254589} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a3d234-e957-5e8d-85be-bdebd0026797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255242} {:answer/id #uuid "edb11fd3-821e-5d36-891b-095d0de53681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6641d652-1c79-5594-8a40-67438b550538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254589} :answer-field/id #uuid "761e5ab1-caf4-5260-859c-054aab437559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256139}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09a46495-b2d7-513a-938f-504451202710" :question/math-academy-id "q-123127" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,j+t[(3\\,i+j)-2\\,j] \\\\  & =2\\,j+t(3\\,i-j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(2-t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t$ and $y=2-t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t)^{2}-(2-t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "711177e4-868e-5ac6-a2b1-961ab8f94f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257775} {:answer/id #uuid "8860493e-bab1-52a0-9bf9-eb7d11745d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257977} {:answer/id #uuid "b78bc20f-293d-5acf-9926-a6d8bb185d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258386} {:answer/id #uuid "db0fb6b5-5dcc-57a4-a8f1-684f9c0bb985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258695} {:answer/id #uuid "f570bf13-b1d2-5f54-853f-bb075d272c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db0fb6b5-5dcc-57a4-a8f1-684f9c0bb985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258695} :answer-field/id #uuid "d19b03b6-7a81-55e6-a56b-0d4882ea6475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259400}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fb44c65-3cac-5280-b910-d92f6181f8ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-123269" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[3\\,j-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)-t\\,i \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1-t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =-i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-t)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536e63b2-339e-55fb-9ef6-16302fa8d80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257495} {:answer/id #uuid "562e0f5a-9f51-526c-b575-21a080e41feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257523} {:answer/id #uuid "67f3bbaf-7696-53c7-a073-517185ea4bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257700} {:answer/id #uuid "714228f8-6684-5ac7-831f-a8ce0050c5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257778} {:answer/id #uuid "83d7a139-b408-5fd1-8726-70337664b912" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "536e63b2-339e-55fb-9ef6-16302fa8d80b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257495} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f23d5c3-adfc-5e83-a5b1-c055960487eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259115}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b87e3ea6-f1f9-5cac-b915-0751187e2e15" :question/math-academy-id "q-123268" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,1)$ to $(1,2).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+j$ and $b=i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t[(i+2\\,j)-(-i+j)] \\\\  & =(-i+j)+t(2\\,i+j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t-1$ and $y=t+1$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=1+x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+(2t-1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1) \\\\  & =2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t+1))\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e37f599-d9fb-59d9-86fe-bf8eaa2a4cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256920} {:answer/id #uuid "1975ac33-0269-5ed1-88f8-8afee74b72b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29{\\int }_{0}^{1}(10t-3)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257001} {:answer/id #uuid "2f82862b-d6d3-54c7-bc2b-fdf11833df09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+7)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257200} {:answer/id #uuid "33c69e8d-5ac9-5a07-ab6f-0a29d6e3e45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257240} {:answer/id #uuid "fbcdea73-c430-5bfe-8af1-0bac80bdcafe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+7)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33c69e8d-5ac9-5a07-ab6f-0a29d6e3e45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257240} :answer-field/id #uuid "05a11e2f-b331-55cb-99aa-ab3cf37cfaf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259043}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12a82c22-3ce2-5dee-95e7-fff601263913" :question/math-academy-id "q-123279" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-3,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-3\\,i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t[(2\\,i+3\\,j)-(-3\\,i+j)] \\\\  & =(-3\\,i+j)+t(5\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(5t-3)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=5t-3$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t-3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =5\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(5t-3)+(1+2t) \\\\  & =12t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\cdot \\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(12t-5)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9653f49-1a01-5acd-8ce6-f3cd8095fdbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387369} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3322db-d223-55b3-b7dc-f9fd758349fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387370} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c61a6c-a91d-50a4-a701-bf4e530a3094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387371} {:answer/id #uuid "d4be85c4-9c1a-5af1-b31a-957bb0b77474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372} {:answer/id #uuid "95f050db-1db6-5212-8a8b-3de82028eac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4be85c4-9c1a-5af1-b31a-957bb0b77474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77fad7f-b6e3-5827-9df5-363a99a7be9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387368}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a142a9-d042-56b2-ba86-b30a7fb0dfd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-123283" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(-3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-3t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-3t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{-3t+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Line Integral Along a Segment in the Cartesian Plane"}]}
